          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>1. Start with the cemetery — not the stone</h2>
            <p>Almost every Southern Utah family that gets a headstone rejected by a sexton made the same mistake: they picked a monument first and asked the cemetery second. Do it the other way.</p>
            <p>Every cemetery in our five-county footprint has section-specific rules for upright versus flush markers, height maximums, base width, granite color, and required concrete apron dimensions. The rules for <a href="/cemeteries/tonaquint-cemetery" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">Tonaquint Cemetery</a>'s block 3 row 4 are not the same as its cremation garden. <a href="/cemeteries/hurricane-city-cemetery" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">Hurricane City Cemetery</a> caps upright height at 42 inches. <a href="/cemeteries/parowan-city-cemetery" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">Parowan</a> is flush-only. <a href="/cemeteries/ivins-city-cemetery" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">Ivins</a> requires a 4-to-6-inch concrete apron and 48-hour advance notice.</p>
            <p>The first thing we do on any inquiry is call the sexton at your cemetery and get the current rules for your specific plot. This step costs you nothing and prevents 100% of the problems the next steps could otherwise cause.</p>
