          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>3. Choose granite — for Southern Utah's climate, not a catalog image</h2>
            <p>This is where a Southern Utah company diverges from what a Northern Utah or online shop will recommend. Our climate is uniquely punishing for stone. Three factors:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>UV.</strong> 300+ sunny days a year at St. George's elevation is among the highest UV loads any American memorial stone faces. Dark polished granites (jet black, midnight black, Dakota Mahogany) hold color and etch contrast for decades. Marble is too soft — we do not recommend marble for the desert. Salt-and-pepper and lighter grays show dust and pollen more visibly.</li>
              <li><strong>Hard water.</strong> Southern Utah's municipal water is among the hardest in the state. Calcium and magnesium leave white mineral crusts on granite from irrigation over-spray and rainfall runoff. Worst on light granites; nearly invisible on jet black. Plan for gentle non-ionic cleaning every 18–24 months. <em>Never use vinegar or household descalers on polished granite — they permanently etch the surface.</em></li>
              <li><strong>Red-rock dust.</strong> Sandblast-carved letters trap dust that accumulates over years. Laser-etched imagery on polished granite is dramatically more forgiving of dust in this environment than deep-cut sandblast on rougher finishes. If you want a photograph or a temple silhouette, laser etch on polished jet black is the durability leader.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Our default recommendation for most Washington County installations is jet black or midnight black granite with a polished face. For families who want the red-rock landscape tone alongside the memorial, Dakota Mahogany is the strong alternative.</p>
          </div>

