            <h2>8. Veteran memorials — the SUNC decision</h2>
            <p>The Southern Utah National Cemetery in Cedar City was dedicated on 10 September 2025. Before it opened, veteran families in Washington County had a five-hour round trip to Fort Douglas in Salt Lake City for a national cemetery burial. Now they have a 45-minute drive.</p>
            <p>Two paths for eligible veterans:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Free VA-issued marker</strong> (granite or bronze, at no cost) — at SUNC or in any private/municipal cemetery. Simpler, quicker, uniform national-cemetery aesthetic. Requires VA Form 40-1330.</li>
              <li><strong>Custom private headstone with a bronze VA medallion</strong> — full design flexibility (temple silhouettes, laser portraits, companion monuments) with VA recognition. For veterans who died on or after November 1, 1990. Requires VA Form 40-1330M.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Read the <a href="/cemeteries/southern-utah-national-cemetery" class="text-bronze font-medium underline hover:text-evergreen transition-colors">Southern Utah National Cemetery family guide</a> for the full decision framework. Whichever path is right for your family, we help — including on the free VA marker path where we make no revenue. It's not a sales upsell to be honest about this.</p>
          </div>

